Z||000034||Predator||06-11-2001||06:24 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> That Mustang would scare the sh*t out of me if I saw it coming down the road. Then I would probably wreak from laughing so hard. You could buy 2 Vipers and have lots of change left over for what that guy wants for the Lambo kit, and the Viper would eat that for lunch  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000035||FieroHarry||06-11-2001||06:56 PM||||I looked at that thing real close when I took the pics at carlisle.<BR>Very nice paint job, way to much money <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I would rather have the real thing....<P>------------------<BR><B>Harry Waggoner</B><BR>1985 GT 264K+ <I>FOCOSEVA,FOCOA</I><BR><B>"Im not driving to fast, Im flying to low!"</B>||||reg||6
Z||000036||rockcrawl||06-11-2001||09:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Well, it says it's got Rayne suspension, and a V8 Archie V8 (or as he put it, "Archi") kit, so I suppose it's mostly Fiero.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There are a few guys building tube frames that accept Fiero drive train and suspension parts. Using one such frame and some Ryane parts, a Getrag from any GM FWD car, and an Archie kit, it is very posible that the car is not at all Fiero. But that trunk area does look suspiciously like a Fiero. I'm not trying to defend the guy, his price is way out of line, but I don't think we should jump all over him for not saying it's Fiero based. It may be more correct to say it is a custom and uses <B>some</B> Fiero parts.<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000037||rogergarrison||06-12-2001||07:51 AM||||Even a full tube frame, custom job like this is worth way under that 175K. I saw it in person too, paint was flawless. Im agreeing on maybe the $50K, maybe slightly more if it is a custom tube frame. It is an obvious kit no matter where you look, but done very well. As for things like 300Z lamps, exotics have always used lots of off the shelf parts to save on costs of self producing them. Did you know some years of Countach used Volkswagen tail light assemblies turned upside down. And GT 40's got their original tail lights from trailer suppliers. I tend to think its just a stretch Fiero frame because of the transverse layout. Most guys doing the better tube frame kits put in the engine longitudinaly with a Getrag transaxle.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781">  <p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000038||AusFiero||06-12-2001||07:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GT:<BR><B>Hey Aussie,  The one on ebay was in a museum located in Florida.  It was supposedly restored back to its original condition by some big time Pontiac guy.  I cant remember his name.  There were 8 convertible T/As in 69.  4 were automatic and 4 were stick.  I wish it was still on ebay for you to check out.  It may be back later.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah I knew there was only 8. thought I read all auto but your probably right. Still damn pricey though. The one available here is Museum quality for $140,000 less. I know another of the authenticated real ones sold for about US$50,000 about a year ago in the USA. <P>The one down here is cheap because at $35,000 US or $70,000 AU no one is prepared to pay $70,000 for it, but in the states I am sure somebody would. Hmmmm maybe I can be his agent on this and make a dollar myself. Anyone wanna buy a real 69 TA convertible in Concourse condition for only US $50,000. Gotta make a cut too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>||||reg||1
